# THE-640 independent source review

Review date: 2026-09-15  
Candidate: `9a1f50b05d5bd62e53837c4ff75ed5f7d0e2b9ef`  
Baseline: `060afad525a14547336e8d2170803a66af877812`

**Disposition: APPROVE source scope. No blocker remains in the revised
candidate.** This is a source-only review; no Cargo/native process or mutation
was performed by this reviewer.

The production change is exactly the two comparator directions in
`crates/thegn-host/src/monitor/procs_view.rs` plus their comment. CPU and RSS
already use ordinary ascending comparisons; Name and PID now do the same, and
the existing shared `desc` reversal therefore makes the down arrow descending
and the up arrow ascending for all four keys. The existing PID tie-break remains
ascending after the primary comparison for CPU, RSS, and Name. Name still uses
the existing lexical `String` comparator.

The ordering is shared by flat rows and tree roots/siblings/cycle leftovers,
so the rendered table body follows the same `ProcRow` order that the signal
handler indexes. The host monitor test drives the actual `c/m/n/p/r` keys,
checks the corresponding headings and arrows, and checks the rows before and
after each direction transition. Its passive refresh fixture changes the
selected process's RSS from 100 to 400 while ascending; the resulting body is
explicitly `[42, 3, 17]`, and the pending confirmation remains bound to
`(pid 17, start_time 100, "pid 17 middle")`.

The pure view tests cover distinct CPU/RSS/Name/PID ordering in both directions,
stable PID ties, tree roots and siblings, filtered ancestry, orphan roots, and
cycle leftovers. The confirmation test opens `x` only and inspects the pending
record; it does not press `y`, call signal dispatch, or introduce a `y` path.
The production diff adds no performance branch, imports, new process feature, or
owner attribution behavior. The only non-production changes are the associated
tests, delivery metadata, audit, and OpenSpec text.

